---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/layout/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import SchemaBreadcrumb from '../components/seo/SchemaBreadcrumb.astro';

const breadcrumbItems = [{ label: 'Our method' }];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Our Calculation Method | Arcania"
  description="How we calculate your Matriz del Destino, with complete transparency: the reduction rule, the derivation of each position, and the 22-arcana model we follow."
  noindex
>
  <SchemaBreadcrumb slot="schema" items={breadcrumbItems} />

  <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbItems} />

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 pb-16 sm:px-6">
    <h1 class="font-heading text-4xl font-bold text-ink sm:text-5xl">Our Method</h1>

    <div class="mt-6 flex flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
      <p>
        Our calculator follows the standard Matriz del Destino calculation method as established by
        Natalia Ladini. We reduce your day, month, and year of birth to numbers from 1 to 22 and
        distribute them across the 12 positions of the octagram. The calculation runs entirely in your
        browser using JavaScript — no data is sent to any server. The full methodology is documented in
        our guides section.
      </p>
      <p>
        Many Matriz del Destino calculators are black boxes: you enter your date, a result appears, and
        there's no way to know how it was reached. We prefer the opposite. This page explains, in as much
        detail as possible, exactly what our calculation engine does — the same one that runs in your
        browser when you use our
        <a href="/matriz-del-destino/" class="font-medium text-primary hover:underline">calculator</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-ink">The model we follow</h2>
      <p>
        We implement the standard 22-arcana model developed by Natalia Ladini, deliberately aiming for
        parity with what you'd see in other recognised calculators of the method — not a variant of our
        own. Every position in your matrix always reduces to a value between 1 and 22, each corresponding
        to one of the major arcana of the tarot.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-ink">The reduction rule</h2>
      <p>
        The base operation of the entire system: if a number exceeds 22, we add its digits until reaching
        a value between 1 and 22 (for example, 27 → 2 + 7 = 9). This same rule is applied consistently at
        every step of the calculation, with no hidden exceptions.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-ink">How the positions are derived</h2>
      <p>
        We start from three base values — your day (Point A), your month (Point B, not reduced, since it
        already runs from 1 to 12), and the reduced sum of your year's digits (Point C). From there, each
        subsequent position is built by adding and reducing earlier positions: Point D is A + B + C;
        Point E — your comfort zone — is A + B + C + D; and so on through the rest of the position tree:
        the ancestral square, your masculine and feminine lines, your karmic tail, your money line, and
        your four purposes. You can see the step-by-step version, with a complete worked example, in our
        guide on
        <a href="/guias/como-calcular-la-matriz-del-destino/" class="font-medium text-primary hover:underline">
          how to calculate the Matriz del Destino
        </a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-ink">What we don't do</h2>
      <p>
        We don't draw arcana at random, we don't apply manual adjustments "to make the result feel
        right," and we don't hide any formula behind a secret proprietary algorithm. The entire
        calculation happens in your own browser — we never send your date of birth to a server — and you
        can verify any result by repeating the process described above by hand.
      </p>

      <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-bold text-ink">Interpretation, not just arithmetic</h2>
      <p>
        The calculation is only half the work. Each position is then combined with the meaning of its
        corresponding arcana, following a position × arcana approach rather than showing isolated numbers
        — the same combinatorial logic we apply on each of our
        <a href="/arcanos/" class="font-medium text-primary hover:underline">arcana pages</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
